        "### Singular Continuous Measures (The Devil's Staircase / Cantor Measure)\n",
        "A measure $\\mu_{\\text{Cantor}}$ can be continuous everywhere ($F$ has no jump discontinuities), yet completely singular with respect to Lebesgue measure ($\\mu_{\\text{Cantor}}$ is concentrated entirely on a set of Lebesgue measure zero).\n",
        "\n",
        "*Construction (Cantor Function):*\n",
        "Let $C_n$ denote the $n$-th stage in the construction of the middle-thirds Cantor set $C = \\bigcap_{n=1}^\\infty C_n \\subset [0, 1]$.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "![Cantor Set](CantorSet.png){width=80% #fig-cantor-set}\n",
        "\n",
        "Define the density functions and sequence of CDFs:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "F_n(x) := \\int_0^x \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^n \\mathbf{1}_{C_n}(t) \\, dt.\n",
        "$${#eq-cantor-fn-def}\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Uniform Limit:** $F_n \\to F_{\\text{Cantor}}$ uniformly on $[0, 1]$.\n",
        "* **Properties of $F_{\\text{Cantor}}$:**\n",
        "  * $F(0) = 0$ and $F(1) = 1$;\n",
        "  * $F$ is continuous and non-decreasing;\n",
        "  * $F'(x) = 0$ almost everywhere with respect to Lebesgue measure (since $F$ is constant on each open interval removed during the Cantor construction);\n",
        "  * $\\mu_{\\text{Cantor}}(\\{x\\}) = 0$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$;\n",
        "  * $\\mu_{\\text{Cantor}}(C) = 1$, even though $m(C) = 0$ (where $m$ is Lebesgue measure).\n",
        "\n",
